<h2>How Goat Milk Production Changes Through the Lactation</h2>
<p>A goat does not produce the same amount every day. Her output follows a predictable <strong>lactation curve</strong> that starts when she kids (or “freshens”):</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Days 1–4: colostrum.</strong> Thick, yellow first milk for the kids. It is not kept for the house.</li>
<li><strong>Weeks 1–8: the climb.</strong> Production rises quickly as the kids nurse and/or you begin milking.</li>
<li><strong>Weeks 6–10: peak.</strong> Daily yield tops out — often 30–50% higher than the season average.</li>
<li><strong>Months 3–8: the long plateau.</strong> A slow, steady decline of a few percent per month in a well-managed doe.</li>
<li><strong>Months 9–10: late lactation.</strong> Yield tapers off and the doe is usually dried off about two months before her next kidding.</li>
</ol>
<p>That is why a single day’s weigh-in can be misleading. A first freshener that gives 5 pounds at week two may well give 8 pounds at week seven. To see a full curve for your breed — and to project a season total from today’s milk weight — use the <a href="/tools/goat-milk-production-estimator/">Goat Milk Production Estimator</a>.</p>

<h2>How Much Milk Does a Goat Produce in a Year?</h2>
<p>Most dairy records use a standard 305-day lactation, which leaves room for a dry period before the next kidding. Multiply an average day by 305 and you get a realistic yearly figure:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Saanen or Alpine:</strong> roughly 2,300–2,900 lb, or about 270–340 gallons.</li>
<li><strong>Nubian or LaMancha:</strong> roughly 1,800–2,300 lb, or about 210–270 gallons.</li>
<li><strong>Nigerian Dwarf:</strong> roughly 700–850 lb, or about 80–100 gallons.</li>
</ul>
<p>Does that are “milked through” without rebreeding can extend production well beyond 305 days at a lower daily level. We explain how that works in <a href="/do-goats-produce-milk-without-being-pregnant/">do goats produce milk without being pregnant</a>.</p>

<h2>What Affects How Much Milk a Goat Produces Per Day?</h2>
<h3>Genetics and milking lines</h3>
<p>Within any breed there is a huge spread. A doe from a line with recorded milk production (DHIR or a milk star) can out-milk an unrecorded doe of the same breed by 50% or more. When buying, ask for the dam’s milk records, not just her photos.</p>
<h3>Age and number of lactations</h3>
<p>First fresheners usually give 20–30% less than they will as mature does. Production typically climbs through the second and third lactations and peaks around ages four to six.</p>
<h3>Feed, water and minerals</h3>
<p>Milk is about 87% water, so a doe that cannot drink freely cannot milk well. High-quality forage — especially alfalfa — supplies the protein and calcium milk production depends on. We cover feeding in depth in <a href="/how-to-increase-goat-milk-production/">how to increase goat milk production</a>.</p>
<h3>Milking frequency</h3>
<p>Twice-a-day milking, 12 hours apart, is the standard for good reason: once-a-day milking typically produces <strong>15–25% less milk per day</strong>. Read more in <a href="/how-often-do-you-milk-a-goat/">how often do you milk a goat</a>.</p>
<h3>Kids on the doe</h3>
<p>If kids are dam-raised, they drink a large share of the milk — often half or more in the first two months. Many keepers separate kids overnight and milk in the morning, sharing the day with the kids.</p>
<h3>Health and stress</h3>
<p>Parasites, mastitis, heat stress, a new herd member or a change in routine can drop milk overnight. A sudden decline is a signal to check the doe, not just the feed bucket.</p>
